      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Nest%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Eagle Nest sits in the Sangre de Cristo Mountains, a sub‑range of the Rockies, just north of the Colorado border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Nest%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "At the north end of Eagle Nest Lake, anglers target kokanee salmon and rainbow trout in the 2,400‑acre reservoir.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Nest%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eagle Nest holds New Mexico’s coldest January, March, April, May, July, and November temperatures, with 140 inches of snow recorded in 1934‑35.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Nest%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village lies along the Enchanted Circle Scenic Byway, a popular route that loops through New Mexico’s high‑country scenery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
